   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to arbitration provisions\nin state contracts",
    "basePrintNo": "S3427",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3427-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 38,
       "fullName": "David Carlucci",
       "imgName": "370_david_carlucci.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 370,
       "sessionMemberId": 1191,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "CARLUCCI"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 22,
       "fullName": "Andrew Gounardes",
       "imgName": "1049_Andrew_Gounardes.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1229,
       "sessionMemberId": 1227,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "GOUNARDES"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §165, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3427\n\nSPONSOR: SALAZAR\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the state finance law, in relation to\narbitration provisions in state contracts\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo withhold state contracts from vendors which prohibit their employees\nfrom bringing discrimination or harassment claims to court.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends the State Finance Law by adding a new Section 112-2(c) which\nprohibits the Comptroller from approving any contract with any vendor\nwhich mandates that an employee or independent contractor performing\nwork under the contract or subcontract resolve through private arbi-\ntration any claim under title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 or any\ntort related to or arising out of discrimination, sexual assault or\nharassment, including assault and battery, intentional infliction of\nemotional distress, false imprisonment, or negligent hiring, super-\nvision, or retention.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2005, Jamie Lee Jones, an independent contractor working for Halli-\nburton in Baghdad made national headlines. Ms. Jones was brutally raped\nby her coworkers and her employment contract stipulated that claims of\nsexual assault could only be heard in private arbitration.\n\nCongress responded by passing legislation similar to this on a federal\nlevel. While we recognize the benefits of arbitration, that benefit must\nbe weighed against the victim's rights to their day in court and the\nrights of the public to know when claims of such a nature are made\nagainst companies that are supported by tax payer dollars.\n\nEach year the State of New York spends hundreds of millions of dollars\non contracts with businesses that are required to meet State standards\nfor procured services. This legislation will ensure that New York will\nnot be selecting or considering companies which have instituted an\nmandatory arbitration clause for its employees.\n\nThis bill provides for a limited exemption for emergency procurement\nfunctions of the State, but otherwise aims to provide workers with the\nability to protect their rights against assault, abuse and harassment by\naccessing our court system. Mandated arbitration agreements and their\nwidely and growing use by the private sector is undermining our system\nof due process and right to trial by jury.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.6457 of 2017 Died in Government Operations Committee\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
